{"uncertainty": 0.25, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "uart_tx: a start pulse during a frame reloads the hold register and corrupts the remaining data bits", "summary": "Conclusion: rtl_bug in the approved RTL under investigation [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1], serving [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. The failure was reproduced upstream: 1 of 4 checks failed, the build succeeded and $finish was reached [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] [run:run-0002].\n\nDEFECT: the file header states the specification: 8N1 framing, LSB first, CLKS_PER_BIT clocks per bit, and 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The always block has an unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;' before the case statement [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. The IDLE branch already loads hold on start. So the outer statement only changes behaviour while busy is high, which is exactly the case the specification says must be ignored.\n\nCAUSALITY (defect, then fault, then failure): (1) start is asserted while state is START, DATA or STOP. (2) The defect overwrites hold with the new data on that clock edge; this is the fault. (3) The state machine ignores the pulse, so state and bit_idx are unaffected. (4) The DATA state drives tx from hold[bit_idx+1], so every bit sent after the pulse comes from the new byte. (5) The frame on the line is a mix of two bytes, which is the visible failure. The fault begins at the clock edge that samples start, a bit or more before the corrupted bit is driven and checked. It does not begin at the cycle where the check fails.\n\nINCORRECTNESS: the specification fixes the in-flight frame's content. An ignored start must leave the frame unchanged, but this code lets it rewrite the frame. Any start pulse in any non-IDLE state is affected, not only the one in the regression. If the pulse arrives during START, all eight data bits come from the new byte.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (not applied here, for the design owner): remove the outer 'if (start) hold <= data;' and keep the load in the IDLE branch only.\n\nRANKED HYPOTHESES AND HOW EACH IS TESTED OR REJECTED:\n(1) rtl_bug, accepted. It is supported by reading the RTL against its own stated specification [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. It is consistent with the triage top hypothesis 'Likely RTL bug' [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1] [run:run-0001]. That classification was only 30% confidence 'unknown_failure', so it is treated as a candidate, not as proof. Refuting evidence would be the new repro (uart_tx_root_cause_repro.v) passing on this RTL, or failing on RTL with the outer hold load removed.\n(2) testbench_bug, rejected provisionally. A wrong expectation in the regression or triage bench could also fail a check. The new repro derives every expected value from the specification: start bit 0, the byte 8'hA5 LSB first, stop bit 1, and no second frame. It does not copy values from RTL output. Refuting evidence would be this repro failing on the clean RTL, which the must-fail scorer runs. Limitation: I did not see the regression log or the upstream triage testbench source, only their summaries [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2].\n(3) infrastructure, rejected. The reproduction built, reached $finish at time 546000 and reported a counted check failure (1 of 4) [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] [run:run-0002]. That pattern is a functional failure, not a tool crash, timeout or build error.\n(4) spec_ambiguity, rejected. The header clause explicitly says an in-flight start is ignored [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. That clause fully determines that the frame must be unchanged. Caveat: the only specification text available to me is that header comment; no separate specification document was among my inputs. If the project's real specification says otherwise, this conclusion must be revisited."}], "files": [{"path": "uart_tx_root_cause_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse during an in-flight frame must not change the frame", "summary": "Targets the approved RTL under investigation [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1] for [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Sequence: reset, then send 8'hA5. During data bit 2, pulse start with 8'h3C. Four checks, with expected values from the specification only: (1) start bit low at mid-bit with busy high; (2) the received byte equals 8'hA5, sampled at mid-bit LSB first; (3) stop bit high; (4) after the frame the line is idle and busy is low for 3 bit times, so the ignored start does not launch a second frame. The design is expected to put 8'h3D on the line instead of 8'hA5, so check 2 fails, matching the 1-of-4 pattern in [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2]. A watchdog guarantees exactly one verdict line. Only this top file reads plusargs and calls $finish.", "entry": "uart_tx_root_cause_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["Expected result of this repro on uart_tx_reload_while_busy.v: '3 passed, 1 failed'. Check 2 should fail with an observed byte of 8'h3D. This comes from tracing the RTL by hand; it has not been simulated by me.", "Expected result on RTL with the outer 'if (start) hold <= data;' removed: all 4 checks pass. Not run by me.", "The plusarg name that carries the run nonce is not stated in my inputs. The testbench tries NIRMAAN_NONCE=, nirmaan_nonce=, NONCE= and nonce= in that order. If the platform uses another name, the verdict line will carry the wrong nonce and the gate will not count it.", "I have not seen regression_fail.log or the source of uart_tx_triage_repro.v. The link between the original regression failure and this defect rests on the triage summaries."], "escalation": null, "notes": "The independent review of this root-cause work still has to be recorded through review.create by a different seat. The review_record [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.e1] covers the design fixture, not this analysis. The repro-fails gate is run by the platform; no result for it is reported here. Timing in the testbench assumes CLKS_PER_BIT=4, set explicitly by parameter override. Sampling happens on falling clock edges in the middle of each bit, and inputs are driven on falling edges to avoid races."}
